Given an n x n matrix where each of the rows and columns is sorted in ascending order, return the kth smallest element in the matrix.

Note that it is the kth smallest element in the sorted order, not the kth distinct element.

Example 1:

Input: matrix = [[1,5,9],[10,11,13],[12,13,15]], k = 8
Output: 13
Explanation: The elements in the matrix are [1,5,9,10,11,12,13,13,15], and the 8th smallest number is 13

Intuition
In a sorted matrix, instead of brute-force scanning all elements,
we can binary search over the value range. For each guess, count how many elements are ≤ that guess — if fewer than k, move right; else, move left.

var kthSmallest = function(matrix, k) {
    let n = matrix.length;
    let left = matrix[0][0], right = matrix[n - 1][n - 1];

    while (left < right) {
        let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
        let count = 0, j = n - 1;

        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (j >= 0 && matrix[i][j] > mid) j--; // Count the numbers which are greater than the mid
            count += (j + 1);
        }

        if (count < k) left = mid + 1;
        else right = mid;
    }

    return left;
};

if (count < k)
    left = mid + 1;
else
    right = mid;
If less than k elements ≤ mid → k-th element is larger

Otherwise → k-th element is ≤ mid

This is a lower-bound binary search

return left;
At the end:

left === right

This value is the smallest number such that at least k elements ≤ it

That is exactly the k-th smallest element



